import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Note
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The stripe package, a plugin: the package's example app file by file, developing with the Stripe
## CLI, how a payment goes, the webhook (signatures, once, out of order), who has paid, the portal,
## testing without Stripe, the tables, safety, and what comes next. Static: paying needs a server.
meta = {
	title: "Stripe",
	description: "Payments and subscriptions for Roc Solid apps through Stripe, as a plugin: hosted Checkout for one-time payments and subscriptions, the Customer Portal, a webhook that keeps the app's database in step (signed, handled once, in any order), and typed answers to who has paid for what.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "example", label: "A first app" },
		{ id: "local", label: "Developing with the Stripe CLI" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How a payment goes" },
		{ id: "webhook", label: "The webhook" },
		{ id: "paid", label: "Who has paid" },
		{ id: "billing-page", label: "A billing page of your own" },
		{ id: "testing", label: "Testing without Stripe" },
		{ id: "tables", label: "The tables" },
		{ id: "safety", label: "Safety" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Status" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/stripe</code>]}
				title="Charge from the first day"
			>
				Stripe's hosted Checkout takes payments and starts subscriptions, its Customer Portal lets people
				change their plan or card or cancel, and a webhook keeps your database in step. Your app asks its own
				tables who has paid. Cards are typed on Stripe's pages, never yours.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				The package handles the parts where small mistakes cost money: webhook signatures, events that come
				twice or late, one customer per person, and a thank-you page that knows before the event arrives.
				Your app keeps its prices (made in Stripe's Dashboard), its pages, and <code>on_event!</code>: what it
				does when someone pays.
			</p>

			<Heading id="example">A first app</Heading>
			<p>
				Each file below is from <code>packages/stripe/example</code>, which sells a subscription (Basic or
				Pro) and an e-book, with the <a href={Paths.ecosystem_auth}>auth package</a>'s dev sign-in. Its tests
				pay every way, on SQLite and Postgres, against a stand-in for Stripe's API.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>1. The plugin and the webhook.</strong> List the plugin, for its tables. The server needs
				<code>STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</code> and <code>STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET</code> to start. Under <code>--dev</code>
				it starts without them, with Stripe off. The webhook is your route (D-116), calling the package's
				handler with your <code>on_event!</code>.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.stripe_example_main.file} source={Sources.stripe_example_main.rocx} />
			<Code title="routes/stripe/webhook/+server.roc" source={"import stripe.Stripe\nimport ../../../Billing\n\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| Stripe.webhook_route!(request, Billing.on_event!)"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<strong>2. Off to Checkout.</strong> A plan's button posts its price to your endpoint, which calls
				<code>Stripe.checkout!</code> and redirects. The mode comes from your plan, never the form. Each user
				gets one Stripe customer, made the first time. A price may be named by its lookup key, set in the
				Dashboard: <code>{"Stripe.price!(\"pro_monthly\")"}</code> is its id, the same key in test and live
				mode.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.stripe_example_checkout.file} source={Sources.stripe_example_checkout.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>3. Back from Checkout.</strong> Stripe redirects as soon as it is paid, often before its
				events. <code>Stripe.checkout_session!</code> fetches the session (by the <code>?session_id=</code> the
				package put in the success URL) and keeps it, so this page already knows.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.stripe_example_done.file} source={Sources.stripe_example_done.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>4. When someone pays.</strong> <code>on_event!</code> gets each event after the package keeps
				it, in the same transaction. Here a bought e-book becomes an order; a failed renewal is logged:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.stripe_example_billing.file} source={Sources.stripe_example_billing.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>5. Pages for those who paid.</strong> Middleware checks <code>Stripe.subscribed!</code> for
				the subscribers' pages and <code>Stripe.paid_for!</code> for the e-book; anyone else goes to the
				pricing page.
			</p>
			<Code title="Middlewares.roc, in part" source={Sources.stripe_example_gates.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Each guards a group (a directory in brackets, which adds nothing to the paths): the Pro page and its
				endpoint are in <code>routes/(subscribers)/</code>, behind its <code>+middleware.roc</code>:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.stripe_example_subscribers.file} source={Sources.stripe_example_subscribers.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<strong>6. The portal.</strong> A button on the account page opens Stripe's Customer Portal, which
				comes back when they are done. Their changes arrive as events.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.stripe_example_portal.file} source={Sources.stripe_example_portal.rocx} />

			<Heading id="local">Developing with the Stripe CLI</Heading>
			<p>
				Use test mode: <code>sk_test_</code> keys, nothing charged, and the test card
				<code>4242 4242 4242 4242</code> (any future date, any CVC). In the Dashboard, in test mode, make your
				products and prices (<code>checkout!</code> takes their <code>price_…</code> ids), and save the
				Customer Portal's settings once: until then, Stripe makes no portal sessions in test mode. Forward
				events with the <a href="https://docs.stripe.com/stripe-cli">Stripe CLI</a>:
			</p>
			<Code
				source={"stripe listen --forward-to localhost:8080/stripe/webhook   # prints the webhook secret: whsec_…\nexport STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_… STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_…\nrocx dev                                                 # in another terminal\nstripe trigger checkout.session.completed                # an event of your own"}
				plain
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				In production, add a webhook endpoint in the Dashboard (Developers, Webhooks) at
				<code>{"<public URL>/stripe/webhook"}</code>, and give the server its signing secret. Send it the
				<code>checkout.session.*</code>, <code>customer.subscription.*</code>, <code>invoice.paid</code>,
				<code>invoice.payment_failed</code>, <code>customer.deleted</code>, <code>charge.refunded</code> and
				<code>charge.dispute.*</code> events. A one-time payment refunded in full or disputed no longer
				counts as paid (a dispute won gives it back). At start the server logs its keys' mode, and warns
				about live keys under <code>--dev</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="how">How a payment goes</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>{"Stripe.checkout!({ user, email, mode, prices, success_url, cancel_url })"}</code> finds or
				makes the user's Stripe customer (your id in its metadata) and a Checkout Session with the user as
				its <code>client_reference_id</code>. After the success page (step 3), events reach the webhook:
				<code>checkout.session.completed</code>, <code>customer.subscription.created</code>,
				<code>invoice.paid</code>. Renewals, failed payments, plan changes and portal cancellations arrive
				the same way later, and <code>Stripe.subscribed!</code> follows them.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Option of <code>checkout!</code></th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>user : Str</code></td><td>your id for whoever pays</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>email : Str</code></td><td>their address, for a customer made now (optional)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>mode : [Payment, Subscription]</code></td><td>once, or every period</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"prices : List({ price : Str, quantity : U64 })"}</code></td><td>Stripe's price ids, and how many</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>success_url</code>, <code>cancel_url</code></td><td>a path of your site, or a URL; the success URL gets <code>{"session_id={CHECKOUT_SESSION_ID}"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>trial_days : U64</code></td><td>a subscription's free days first (optional)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>promotion_codes : Bool</code></td><td>whether a promotion code may be typed (optional)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"metadata : List((Str, Str))"}</code></td><td>more of your own, on the session (optional)</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="webhook">The webhook</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>{"Stripe.webhook_route!(request, on_event!)"}</code> answers <code>POST /stripe/webhook</code>
				from your route file, behind its folder's middleware, which must let Stripe's servers in
				(<code>webhook_at</code> tells the plugin the path, for its log line at start). For each request:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The signature.</strong> <code>Stripe-Signature</code> must hold the HMAC-SHA256, under your
					webhook secret, of its timestamp and the exact body, at most five minutes old; anything else is a
					400. While you roll a secret, Stripe signs with each; one match is enough.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Once.</strong> The event's id is recorded in the transaction that handles it. A repeat
					(Stripe resends until answered) is answered, not handled again; two copies at once wait for each
					other.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Kept, then yours.</strong> The package keeps what the event says (a checkout's state, a
					subscription's, a deleted customer), then gives <code>on_event!</code> the event, read: its type,
					its user, and <code>Session</code>, <code>Subscription</code>, <code>Invoice</code> or
					<code>Other</code>. Its <code>Err</code> rolls everything back and answers 500, so Stripe tries again
					later, for up to three days.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>In any order.</strong> Stripe promises none. A subscription's row keeps the version, time and
					kind of the event that last changed it, so a late event never brings back an older state, and an
					ended subscription stays ended. For two changes in one second, the package asks Stripe which is last.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Note>
				Keep slow work out of <code>on_event!</code>: queue a <a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>job</a> for the
				receipt or the email, and it commits with the event.
			</Note>

			<Heading id="paid">Who has paid</Heading>
			<p>
				These read your database, never Stripe: a page that checks them waits on no other server, and nothing
				the browser sends decides who has paid.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Says</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Stripe.subscribed!(user)</code></td><td>whether they have a subscription that is active, trialing, or past due while Stripe tries the card again</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Stripe.paid_for!(user, what)</code></td><td>whether such a subscription has a price or product (<code>price_…</code>, <code>prod_…</code>), or a paid one-time payment was for the price and hasn't been refunded in full or disputed since</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Stripe.subscription!(user)</code></td><td>their subscription: status, items (each price's amount, currency, interval and lookup key: what this subscriber pays), when it renews or ends, whether it ends then</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Stripe.payments!(user)</code></td><td>their one-time payments that were paid, and not refunded in full or disputed since</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="billing-page">A billing page of your own</Heading>
			<p>
				The portal (<code>{"Stripe.portal!(user, return_url)"}</code>, its URL, or <code>NoCustomer</code>)
				does everything a subscriber may change. To show billing on a page of your own instead, in your
				words, these ask Stripe each time (read them on that page, not on every page):
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Stripe.card!(user)</code></td><td>the card their subscription is charged to (brand, last four digits, expiry): the subscription's, else their customer's default; <code>NoCard</code> for none</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Stripe.invoices!(user)</code></td><td>their invoices, the newest first, each with its number, status, amount, page and PDF</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Stripe.cancel!(user)</code></td><td>their subscription ends at its period's end (a trial's end, before the first charge), and <code>subscription!</code> says so at once</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Stripe.keep!(user)</code></td><td>undoes that: it goes on, charged as before</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Stripe.card_portal!(user, return_url)"}</code></td><td>the portal opened at changing the card, coming back once it is changed: cards are typed on Stripe's pages</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				For the rest of Stripe's API (a refund, a plan changed), <code>Stripe.post!</code> and
				<code>Stripe.get!</code> call it as the package does: the secret key, a pinned
				<code>Stripe-Version</code>, an idempotency key on a create, a retry after a network error. That key
				is new each call. When the app may ask twice (a job run again, a form sent twice),
				<code>Stripe.post_with!</code> takes your own key, so Stripe creates once.
			</p>

			<Heading id="testing">Testing without Stripe</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>STRIPE_API_URL</code> points the package at another server. The example's tests start a
				stand-in for Stripe's API (<code>example/test/stand-in.mjs</code>, Node with no dependencies): the
				endpoints the package calls, answering with Stripe's objects, and what paying on Checkout's page
				would do (a subscription on a Visa ending 4242, with its first invoice), or a month's bill, or a
				card saved in the portal. It keeps every request it was sent, for a test to look at. Each test
				signs an event as Stripe does and sends it to the webhook. Copy it into your app's tests.
			</p>

			<Heading id="tables">The tables</Heading>
			<p>
				The plugin's migrations (<code>stripe/0001_stripe</code> to <code>stripe/0003_forgotten</code>) make
				five tables, the same on SQLite and Postgres: <code>stripe_customers</code> (each user's Stripe
				customer), <code>stripe_checkouts</code>, <code>stripe_subscriptions</code> (as the events last said),
				<code>stripe_events</code> (those handled, swept after 30 days) and <code>stripe_forgotten</code>
				(customers whose users were deleted). The package keeps no users: it knows them by your app's id.
			</p>
			<p>
				Deleting someone, <code>{"Stripe.forget!(user)"}</code> deletes their customer at Stripe, which ends
				their subscription there at once (no refund), then their rows here. Their invoices stay at Stripe, as
				tax law wants. Stripe's events about that customer afterwards are taken and kept nowhere, so they
				can't bring the rows back.
			</p>

			<Heading id="safety">Safety</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>No card reaches your server: Checkout and the portal are Stripe's pages.</li>
				<li>The webhook takes signed events only, compared in constant time, at most five minutes old.</li>
				<li>A checkout belongs to its user: the success page refuses another user's session.</li>
				<li>Every create carries an idempotency key, so a retried request makes one customer, one session.</li>
				<li>Test and live keys are told apart at start, and live keys under <code>--dev</code> are a warning.</li>
			</ul>
			<Note>
				A <code>routes/+middleware.roc</code> that requires sign-in everywhere would refuse Stripe's webhook
				too: guard your routes by directory instead. The webhook has no <code>Origin</code>, so the
				platform's cross-site check lets it through.
			</Note>

			<Heading id="status">Status</Heading>
			<p>
				Built and tested: Checkout (payments, subscriptions, trials, promotion codes), the Customer Portal,
				the webhook, the checks for who has paid, refunds and disputes taking back a one-time payment, and a
				billing page of your own (the card, the invoices, cancelling and keeping). Not yet, in the package's
				<code>PROPOSALS.md</code>: Stripe Tax, metered billing, Connect, embedded Checkout and changing a plan
				from the app.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
